        revenueText: "**Animal APIs were 95% of Q1 FY27 revenue.** The company does not publish a split by market; most sales go to unregulated markets in India, Asia-Pacific and Latin America, with Europe small and the US not yet started. **Top 10 customers were 38% of Q1 FY27 sales (29% in Q4) and top 10 products 68%**, so concentration is rising. Management aims for ₹150cr-plus a quarter; Q1 FY27 was ₹139cr (+34%).",
        mixes: [
            { caption: 'Revenue by business, Q1 FY27 (company)', parts: [{ label: 'Animal APIs', share: 95 }, { label: 'Other', share: 5 }] },
        ],
        customers: ["**Veterinary formulators in India, Asia, Latin America and Africa**, buying on orders and some fixed-price contracts. It added 47 customer registrations in one quarter, counting each country separately.", "**European animal-health companies**, served under 3 approved European certificates (CEPs), with 3 more expected by end-2026.", "**US formulators (not yet)**: 5 US veterinary master files are filed and 6 more planned. The world's five largest animal-health companies are not yet customers."],
        difficulty: { level: 'Medium', text: "Veterinary ingredients are simpler than niche human APIs, and unregulated buyers ask for little paperwork, which is why about 20 Indian and 40 Chinese makers compete. Regulated markets are harder: each product needs a master file (a confidential dossier telling regulators how it is made), and the plant must pass US FDA and European inspections." },
        moat: "In unregulated markets little stops a buyer switching to a cheaper Chinese supplier. Once a US or European customer names NGL in its approval, switching needs a refiling, but that moat is still being built.",
        salesDrivers: ["**Chinese pricing**: Chinese makers set the price. They held prices in March–April despite higher costs, then started raising them from May, which the MD called “a big relief”.", "**Range and reach**: about 45 ingredients (9–10 added a year) and better penetration in Latin America drove FY26's 36% growth.", "**Capacity**: Phase 1 is 70–80% used and NGL outsources about 20% of its intermediates. Phase 2 at Tarapur (five clean rooms) commissions in early Q2 FY27, with commercial output from H2 and ₹350–400cr of peak revenue over 3–4 years.", "**Regulatory approvals**: Europe from the second half of FY27 and the US from FY28, depending on when a customer files and the FDA inspects. Management expects regulated sales to earn 3–5 points more margin."],
        inputs: "**Chemical intermediates and solvents**, part made in-house and about 20% of intermediate needs outsourced. Materials were 48% of sales in FY26 and have been 47–51% for five years. Power and factory costs were 18%.",
        costEffect: "**Partly passed on, with a lag.** Part of its sales are on fixed-price contracts, so the Q4 FY26 jump in freight and raw-material costs could not be passed on at once; partial price increases went through in Q1 FY27. **Rule of thumb:** a 10% rise in material costs not passed on would cut the margin by about 5 points (16% to 11%). **What happened:** its real inputs have no Indian price index, so the chart uses India's acetic-acid price index as a stand-in for chemical inputs; it rose about 7% from Q1 FY24 to Q1 FY27, while the margin moved from 14% to 17%. The real driver was Chinese oversupply: selling prices fell through 2024–25 and the margin dropped to about 6% in the second half of FY25, then recovered with volume and firmer prices.",
        competitors: [
            { name: 'Chinese veterinary API makers (about 40)', note: 'Set prices in most of its products and hold large capacity; the MD says oversupply will take years to clear.' },
            { name: 'Indian veterinary API makers (about 20), such as Sequent Scientific', note: 'Compete in the same unregulated markets; competition is “the same” as before, per the MD.' },
            { name: 'Established US and European suppliers', note: 'Regulated buyers usually already have two suppliers; NGL pitches itself as a non-Chinese extra source.' },
        ],
        position: "**A mid-sized Indian veterinary API maker** with about 45 products; its top 10 customers rose to 38% of sales in Q1 FY27. Phase 1 of its new plant runs at 70–80%.",
        money: "Screener shows **₹118cr of net debt** (₹123cr of borrowings less ₹5cr of cash), up from ₹34cr in FY24 and above the ₹95cr “worst case” management gave in November 2025; it also holds ₹39cr of investments. Rated BBB+ by CRISIL and ICRA. Phase 2 capex rose from ₹160cr to ₹210cr (higher metal prices and more automation), with ₹182.75cr spent by March 2026. Ongoing capex is about ₹15–20cr a year, and a brownfield expansion is planned for FY28. No equity raised. Promoters hold **72.5%**, little changed from 73.1%. In FY26 the company and its promoters paid SEBI about ₹67 lakh to settle wrong shareholding disclosures from 2002 to 2019, and its two executive directors' profit commissions rose 2.6 times.",
        watch: ["A US customer filing that triggers the FDA inspection.", "The margin holding inside 15–18% as Phase 2 costs start.", "Borrowings falling once Phase 2 is paid for."],
        charts: pharmaApiCharts['NGL Fine-Chem'],
